When a deleted WebGL program or shader is passed to a function, Ladybird
generates INVALID_VALUE, and INVALID_OPERATION for any other deleted
object. WebKit’s WebGLRenderingContextBase::validateWebGLObject() picks
the same two errors by type, and Gecko’s ObjectJS::ErrorOnDeleted() is
INVALID_VALUE for programs and shaders. Blink does the same, in its
ValidateWebGLProgramOrShader(), for every program and shader function
but useProgram(), which goes through ValidateWebGLObject() instead and
generates INVALID_OPERATION. Ladybird follows Gecko and WebKit there,
and the GLES rule for program and shader names.

The test’s output matches Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5 line for line, and
Chrome 158 in every line but useProgram()’s.
